<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · 1973 · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ya Rayah</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ar" dir="rtl">يا رايح</bdi> / <bdi lang="ar-Latn">Yâ râyiḥ</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An Algerian song from 1973 about leaving home and longing to return, sung around the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Dahmane El Harrachi (Amrani Abderrahmane)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Dahmane El Harrachi (Amrani Abderrahmane)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Algerian chaâbi; Algerian emigrants in France</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic (Algerian)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Algeria</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1973</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words and music by Dahmane El Harrachi (1973))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ya-rayah">Ya Rayah</a>&quot; (&quot;you, the one leaving&quot;) is an Algerian chaâbi song written and performed in 1973 by Dahmane El Harrachi, whose name was Amrani Abderrahmane. It belongs to the chaâbi and Andalusian tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. It is a ballad of the traveller and the exile, of the emigrant and the longing to come back, and that theme has given it a wide appeal.</p><p>In 1993 the French-Algerian singer Rachid Taha recorded it on his second album, which bore his own name. He released it as a single in August 1997, from the compilation album Carte Blanche; it reached number eleven on the French singles chart and was certified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in February 1998. It also appeared on his album Diwân (1998). In September 1998 Taha sang it with the Algerian singers Khaled and Faudel at the Palais omnisports de Paris-Bercy, and their version appeared on the live album 1,2,3 Soleils the following year. In 2003 he sang it as a duet with Enrico Macias at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. After Taha's death the song returned to the French top 20.</p><p>It has been sung in many languages, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, Hindi, Turkish, Greek, Russian and Serbian. Its tune was used in the Bollywood film Mann (1999), and it was sampled in an Israeli rap song in 2010. In 2012 the Algerian singer Amel Bouchoucha recorded it with African dance beats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> sounds.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of exile and emigration, about leaving home and the longing to return. The mood is melancholy and reflective.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="2950542" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn sg-genius-btn-en" data-genius="9835034" data-what="English translation">Show the English translation from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics and their translation in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Dahmane-el-harrachi-ya-rayah-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a> &middot; <a href="https://genius.com/Genius-english-translations-dahmane-el-harrachi-oh-traveler-english-translation-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its English translation</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ya Rayah (Rachid Taha)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1993</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover; single 1997</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ya Rayah (Rachid Taha, Khaled and Faudel, on 1,2,3 Soleils)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1998</span><span class="sg-chain-h">live performance</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ki an se thelo (George Dalaras)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Greek version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ya Rayah (Dobranotch)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Russian version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jako, jako slabo srce zavodiš (Zdravko Čolić)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Serbian version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kali Nagin Ke Jaisi (Sanjeev Darshan; sung by Udit Narayan and Kavita Krishnamurthy, in the film Mann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1999</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Belyby (Booksills, featuring Shlome Shabat, on TACT)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2010</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sampled</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ya Rayah (Amel Bouchoucha)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2012</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">J'suis Comme Toi (Ya Rayah) (John Mamann with Lartiste and Rim'K)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">interpolation</span></li></ul></section>
